# How many land animals a player's area can hold, by biome and season.
#
# Respawning Animals fills the world up to a cap of land animals per player
# (15 by default, the min_animals_near_player gamerule). Without this file
# every place gets that same 15. With it, the cap becomes 15 x a fraction
# that depends on where the player is and what season it is.
#
# How the fraction is worked out, for each biome and season:
#   1. Add up (spawn weight x average group size x the seasonal chance from
#      the other .rules files) for every land animal the biome can spawn.
#      That is the biome's "creature weight".
#   2. Divide by the creature weight of the reference place below. That
#      gives a fraction. Richer than the reference is cut back to 1.0.
#   3. Multiply by any row below. Rows are for places where step 1 is wrong.
# A player's cap is the average over 9 spots around them, 64 blocks apart.
# Ocean spots are skipped, since they have no land animals.
#
# Warning: spawn weights are not animal counts. They are odds that mod
# authors tuned against each other, so some biomes will come out too rich
# or too poor. Run `/seasonalspawns capacity` to see the table. Fix what looks
# wrong with a row. A row multiplies the result, 0 to 2, and the final
# fraction is still capped at 1.0.
#
# Delete this whole file to turn the feature off. The game's own cap is
# then used everywhere.

[capacity]
reference  = minecraft:forest summer
floor      = 3
confidence = guess
why        = The reference is summer forest because it is the richest ordinary place:
             Naturalist, Ambient Creatures and the farm animals all spawn there. Every
             other place is measured against it. The floor of 3 keeps a desert or a
             winter tundra from going empty. Both numbers are balance calls.
#                 spring summer autumn winter
# The audit of the first table (2026-10): frozen ocean scored 0.65 and ice spikes
# 0.78 of a summer forest. That is the penguin: weight 50 and groups of 10, so one
# animal adds 500 to the sum. Real polar country is poor in land animals, and the
# fraction should say so. Everything else in the table looked plausible: rainforest
# and savanna richest, forest next, then plains, taiga and mountains, then desert.
polar = 0.6  0.6  0.6  0.6
